T1  - Milk Powder Co-Supplemented with Inulin and Resistant Dextrin Improves Glycemic Control and Insulin Resistance in Elderly Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus: A 12-Week Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled Trial.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1002/mnfr.201800865
A1  - Cai, Xiaxia
A1  - Yu, Huanling
A1  - Liu, Lan
A1  - Lu, Tong
A1  - Li, Jingjie
A1  - Ji, Yacheng
A1  - Le, Zhiyin
A1  - Bao, Lei
A1  - Ma, Weiwei
A1  - Xiao, Rong
A1  - Yang, Yuexin
Y1  - 2018//
Y2  - 20181203//
N2  - SCOPE: The objective of the present study is to evaluate the effects of milk powder co-supplemented with inulin and resistant dextrin (MPCIR) on elderly patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM)., METHODS AND RESULTS: A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical trial is carried out among elderly T2DM patients. The subjects recruited from the community are randomly assigned to either the MPCIR group or placebo group for 12 weeks intervention. Each group receives 45 g milk powder with or without inulin and resistant dextrin. Anthropometric and metabolic variables are measured. For the MPCIR group, systolic blood pressure (BP) and diastolic BP are reduced significantly by 5.45 and 4.56 mm Hg (p < 0.001, vs placebo group), respectively. Compared with the placebo group, the fasting and 2-h postprandial plasma glucose levels, glycosylated serum protein, and insulin resistance index of the MPCIR group are significantly decreased by 0.96 mmol L-1 , 1.47 mmol L-1 , 16.33 mumol L-1 , and 0.65 respectively (p < 0.001). The MPCIR group shows an increase by 7.09 muIU mL-1 and 20.43 in 2-h postprandial insulin (p = 0.016) and beta-cell function index (p < 0.001), respectively., CONCLUSION: MPCIR supplementation helps to improve glycemic control, insulin resistance, and blood pressure. Copyright © 2018 WILEY-VCH Verlag GmbH & Co. KGaA, Weinheim.
