T1  - Mometasone furoate cream reduces acute radiation dermatitis in patients receiving breast radiation therapy: results of a randomized trial.
T3  - Comment in: Int J Radiat Oncol Biol Phys. 2014 Nov 15;90(4):736-8 PMID: 25585778 [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/25585778] Erratum in: Int J Radiat Oncol Biol Phys. 2015 Mar 15;91(4):882 PMID: 25752410 [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/25752410]
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ijrobp.2014.06.033
A1  - Hindley, Andrew
A1  - Zain, Zakiyah
A1  - Wood, Lisa
A1  - Whitehead, Anne
A1  - Sanneh, Alison
A1  - Barber, David
A1  - Hornsby, Ruth
Y1  - 2014//
Y2  - 20141018//
N1  - Comment in (CIN), Erratum in (EIN)
N2  - PURPOSE: We wanted to confirm the benefit of mometasone furoate (MF) in preventing acute radiation reactions, as shown in a previous study (Bostrom et al, Radiother Oncol 2001;59:257-265)., METHODS AND MATERIALS: The study was a double-blind comparison of MF with D (Diprobase), administered daily from the start of radiation therapy for 5 weeks in patients receiving breast radiation therapy, 40 Gy in 2.67-Gy fractions daily over 3 weeks. The primary endpoint was mean modified Radiation Therapy Oncology Group (RTOG) score., RESULTS: Mean RTOG scores were significantly less for MF than for D (P=.046). Maximum RTOG and mean erythema scores were significantly less for MF than for D (P=.018 and P=.012, respectively). The Dermatology Life Quality Index (DLQI) score was significantly less for MF than for D at weeks 4 and 5 when corrected for Hospital Anxiety and Depression (HAD) questionnaire scores., CONCLUSIONS: MF cream significantly reduces radiation dermatitis when applied to the breast during and after radiation therapy. For the first time, we have shown a significantly beneficial effect on quality of life using a validated instrument (DLQI), for a topical steroid cream. We believe that application of this cream should be the standard of care where radiation dermatitis is expected. Crown Copyright © 2014. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
