ID  - 26445629
T1  - Effects of Probiotics on Biomarkers of Oxidative Stress and Inflammatory Factors in Petrochemical Workers: A Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled Trial.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.4103/2008-7802.164146
A1  - Mohammadi, Ali Akbar
A1  - Jazayeri, Shima
A1  - Khosravi-Darani, Kianoush
A1  - Solati, Zahra
A1  - Mohammadpour, Nakisa
A1  - Asemi, Zatollah
A1  - Adab, Zohre
A1  - Djalali, Mahmoud
A1  - Tehrani-Doost, Mehdi
A1  - Hosseini, Mostafa
A1  - Eghtesadi, Shahryar
Y1  - 2015//
Y2  - 20150901//
N2  - BACKGROUND: The aim of the current study was to determine effects of probiotic yoghurt and multispecies probiotic capsule supplementation on biomarkers of oxidative stress and inflammatory factors in petrochemical workers., METHODS: This randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial was done among petrochemical workers. Subjects were randomly divided into three groups to receive 100 g/day probiotic yogurt (n = 12) or one probiotic capsule daily (n = 13) or 100 g/day conventional yogurt (n = 10) for 6 weeks. The probiotic yoghurt was containing two strains of Lactobacillus acidophilus and Bifidobacterium lactis with a total of min 1 x 10(7) CFU. Multispecies probiotic capsule contains seven probiotic bacteria spices Actobacillus casei 3 x 10(3), L. acidophilus 3 x 10(7), Lactobacillus rhamnosus 7 x 10(9), Lactobacillus bulgaricus 5 x 10(8), Bifidobacterium breve 2 x 10(10), Bifidobacterium longum 1 x 10(9) and Streptococcus thermophilus 3 x 10(8) CFU/g. Fasting blood samples were obtained at the beginning and end of the trial to quantify biomarkers of oxidative stress and inflammatory factors., RESULTS: Although a significant within-group decrease in plasma protein carbonyl levels was seen in the probiotic capsule group (326.0 +/- 308.9 vs. 251.0 +/- 176.3 ng/mL, P = 0.02), the changes were similar among the three groups. In addition, significant within-group decreases in plasma iso prostaglandin were observed in the probiotic supplements group (111.9 +/- 85.4 vs. 88.0 +/- 71.0 pg/mL, P = 0.003) and in the probiotic yogurt group (116.3 +/- 93.0 vs. 92.0 +/- 66.0 pg/mL, P = 0.02), nevertheless there were no significant change among the three groups., CONCLUSIONS: Taken together, consumption of probiotic yogurt or multispecies probiotic capsule had beneficial effects on biomarkers of oxidative stress in petrochemical workers.
